.TH conv "" "Command"
.SH NAME
conv \- convert between various numeric bases and ASCII
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBconv [\fInumber\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
conv -- convert between various numeric bases and ASCII
Usage:	conv [number]
If no `number' is found, numbers are one per line on standard input
.HE
.B conv
converts
.I number
and prints it as a hexadecimal, decimal, octal, and binary value,
followed by its low-order byte as a quoted character, all on one
line.
If no
.I number
is given on the command line,
.B conv
reads lines from the standard input and converts each nonblank line
the same way, until end of file.
.PP
.I number
may be written with an optional leading
.B \-
for a negative value, followed by one of the prefixes below:
.DS
.ta 12n
\fB0x\fR or \fB0X\fR	a hexadecimal value
\fB#\fR	also introduces a hexadecimal value
\fB0\fR	(no following x or X) introduces an octal value
\fB$\fR	introduces a binary value
.DE
A prefix consisting of a quote character followed by one other
character gives the ASCII code of that other character as the
value.
.PP
With none of the above prefixes, the number is read as decimal.
Embedded blanks and tabs within the digits are ignored; hexadecimal
digits may be given in either case.
.PP
In the character field of the output,
.B conv
prints the value's low-order byte in quotes.
If the byte's high bit is set,
.B conv
prints a leading
.B ~
and clears the bit before printing the character.
If the resulting code is a control character or DEL,
.B conv
prints a leading
.B ^
and the corresponding visible letter (DEL is shown as
.BR ^? ).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B conv
prints
.B "bad digit"
and discards the current number if a digit outside the selected
base is found.
